          <p style="font-size: 11px; color: var(--fg-muted);">
            A model that always predicts the average risk has a Brier score of about 0.027,
            so the Brier score shows no gain over that baseline. Treat probabilities as rankings
            until recalibrated.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="subgroup-panel">
          <h4>Subgroups</h4>
          <p>No-CKD: AUROC 0.753; CKD: AUROC 0.571 (CI 0.09–1.00, only 2 deaths).</p>
          <p>Female: AUROC 0.684; Male: AUROC 0.750.</p>
          <p>Intervals overlap and are very wide, so I cannot claim equal performance, only that the data are too thin to say.</p>
